## 5. The `t<=1.844` exclusion is used with the correct quantifier

The hostile audit of the phase-resolved remainder theorem proves that the stated
full-core/no-container branch has no sufficiently large survivor with

\[
t={q-k\over D}\le1.844.
\]

The candidate does not infer a uniform positive distance from the endpoint.  It uses
only the adverse closed-endpoint consequence

\[
\liminf {q-k\over D}\ge1.844.
\]

This remains valid for a sequence approaching `1.844` from above.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 6. The asymptotic chamber floor follows without rate assumptions

Canonical full-core Type-II asymptotics give

\[
{p\over D}\to\pi,
\qquad
{\sqrt{R_4}\over D}\to\sqrt{\Delta_2}.
\]

Combining the exact interface identity with the two lower-limit floors gives

\[
\liminf {H_D\over D}
\ge
1.844+(\rho+0.007707)-\pi+2\sqrt{\Delta_2}.
\]

Then the finite Section-85 inequality yields

\[
\liminf {A_{ch}-a_0Z_{ch}\over D}
\ge
{\sqrt3\over4}
\left[
1.844+\rho+0.007707-\pi+2\sqrt{\Delta_2}
\right].
\]

No convergence rate for `p`, `R_4`, or `q-k` is required.

**Verdict:** PASS.

