## 8. Positive-core corollary is algebraically consistent

Section 67 gives

\[
m=R_4+f+(q-k)+1.
\]

The exact interface identity also gives

\[
H_D=2[(q-k)+f]-p.
\]

Therefore

\[
m-R_4={p+H_D\over2}+1
={q-k+\tau_D+2\sqrt{R_4}\over2}+1.
\]

The same lower-limit inputs yield

\[
\liminf {m-R_4\over D}>2.247708.
\]

The companion certificate checks this weaker rounded corollary as well.

**Verdict:** PASS.

---

## 9. What the theorem does not prove

The result does not imply a macroscopic inactive chamber.  The quantity
`A_ch-a0 Z_ch` is the canonical Oler-weighted chamber surplus, and linear surplus can
still live in thin doubled near-geodesic corridors or near-degenerate exceptional
faces.  The Section-85 regression remains binding.

It also does not address the separate Type-I macroscopic-container/exterior-cap branch,
and it does not promote the proof-only `g=0.007707` artifact to canonical theorem-state
authority.

No strict same-cardinality competitor or exact unit equilateral triangle is produced.

**Final verdict:** **PASS.**  On the current modern scope-safe stack, every remaining
full-core Type-II survivor has train slack at least `0.7467921D-o(D)`, Section-85
interface at least `1.353825D-o(D)`, chamber surplus at least `0.586223D-o(D)`, and
positive-core excess at least `2.247708D-o(D)`.  C-II and Level I remain open.
